#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bf804d6b-ceba-558b-8cde-11d70906372f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3739" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "803d8981-7cc1-5f4a-bb47-21d7583e00ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-3742" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3b585a-909a-5498-8c39-d8d03e378d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-3746" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/id #uuid "ed7d94d2-25c5-53d9-830e-a659d304ecb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3747" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41aa9c73-5af6-5b30-be41-696e1204b901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298897} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bdf0f5-b3fe-5471-a098-cea3d66ac947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298898} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f384a9-2f74-52c8-882c-570343ba1435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298899} {:answer/id #uuid "4b780da8-cbf8-5cb3-b18c-a90e9b11bf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298900} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ade8a1-544b-55c3-aba7-3741f93d9a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b780da8-cbf8-5cb3-b18c-a90e9b11bf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a45ea804-c24e-5861-a32d-d80d7817241b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "444031a3-3309-59e7-a714-c81365022d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-114402" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c5f5e2-1cb0-5541-a8b5-2ef8cfe8fedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298904} {:answer/id #uuid "222934c1-158c-5d7c-bed8-961584e11904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298905} {:answer/id #uuid "b540a420-c4e6-51d4-86d2-bcac80d042f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298906} {:answer/id #uuid "51526fb8-c340-5715-8138-057213541ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298907} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0a9a2f-073f-5bbc-b545-28af755c893b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b540a420-c4e6-51d4-86d2-bcac80d042f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298906} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0b0df6-26c0-5280-9e76-20c7d65d1bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61ca6f40-387d-5020-96de-c8055fd09d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114404" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/0c9e74029cf6761469c72dc76dc6b13ba92107857c2a2d63ca6983dec282db37.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is not a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a329ca-e000-513c-a624-fe32c9f29396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093760} {:answer/id #uuid "0774774a-5064-5ba7-8b6d-ab0d82cb1f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093761} {:answer/id #uuid "1f819151-c5cf-5b4d-8903-0cab4d515534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093762} {:answer/id #uuid "2141b62a-7e3d-59eb-9feb-9ef7a74970db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093763} {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a4b055-23cf-5535-b288-ddb6c6e34d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c163d924-6190-52ae-9f3b-628d336cfc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-114407" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8dcdd9-07a4-5751-b163-141ec66cb107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298911} {:answer/id #uuid "f35f1df9-77b9-5fe4-bb1a-511f3b9d2549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298912} {:answer/id #uuid "1100cc7f-aaa6-55ff-b1c7-2017629861cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298913} {:answer/id #uuid "7b123f5f-7687-556d-bb81-fd29f6f16416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298914} {:answer/id #uuid "4662a91e-0f0c-514d-ab7d-d9d9d14d1bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4662a91e-0f0c-514d-ab7d-d9d9d14d1bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298915} :answer-field/id #uuid "5efe734d-5228-5332-aacd-08464c673489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5145ec8c-5097-582c-b02c-15f0c24181cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-114413" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7)+{e}^{x}(2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+{e}^{x}(2x-2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)>0$ for all real values of $x,$ the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e7456b2-074f-5fe7-8eb4-44865d674201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298918} {:answer/id #uuid "91e3d238-3616-50fa-861b-91e6c1dcebd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298919} {:answer/id #uuid "71a97db9-7012-5fa0-9cf5-70ef868887a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298920} {:answer/id #uuid "169abb35-6522-5029-ab94-aaf688833eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298921} {:answer/id #uuid "d71d9e2a-d68e-56b2-b7c6-7b4a6d009d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e3d238-3616-50fa-861b-91e6c1dcebd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ae4f4c4-6699-5a4a-8667-b6e7ae1f07d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c94ce91c-0982-58bc-9de3-2473b47fabe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-114416" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/826336bca267300c9b98e43072f74bb5efdacff0a4007fb7b938eb61893d8fbe.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbda536a-a30e-52c7-884c-a13f44e27330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298925} {:answer/id #uuid "746a2a7e-fc82-541e-8977-6ce74600fb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298926} {:answer/id #uuid "3be0c5ab-7caf-5603-aed2-ea5f3e51ee1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298927} {:answer/id #uuid "29f51c33-ab49-51a4-a3e7-528fc07c38b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298928} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8b9b7e-24b0-5f32-a79b-8c280d688d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be0c5ab-7caf-5603-aed2-ea5f3e51ee1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298927} :answer-field/id #uuid "278ad05f-436c-5367-87c0-9d7cb55a4dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19c35970-5e9e-5ce0-9a18-4aeadc55467f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127112" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/885f9b9ea95909edf81c756397bff73ca8a83619117f51c9db7433c5082cf354.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=h(x)$ above. What is the point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=-1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$   the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=-1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90ca144-d79e-50cf-8d47-03c0479ec34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095031} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8df573-c13f-5589-a3e4-38a7fd4afa0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095032} {:answer/id #uuid "556a65ca-a4a0-5263-b495-f4edf0b6d408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095033} {:answer/id #uuid "63c5c382-e954-57c7-9619-2fda2a9a84f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095034} {:answer/id #uuid "7a098f52-985a-50ea-baa8-722f1048e871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63c5c382-e954-57c7-9619-2fda2a9a84f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095034} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5dd35d-30e6-5b53-bd0c-23c6810c7491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c4b4867-1510-53f9-af59-23034ea3f47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-20850" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-2x.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}=12>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba25312-794c-546b-8978-1447f36282ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298932} {:answer/id #uuid "a9205c82-6fd0-5c53-95e6-38718a4f2164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298933} {:answer/id #uuid "79fc881a-1905-50fa-a4e8-3d4d0c9c011e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298934} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7c21d3-47fc-59b0-9678-4719bc0e838e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298935} {:answer/id #uuid "ee95f8d3-6d03-5a52-9899-381aeba3d399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba25312-794c-546b-8978-1447f36282ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298932} :answer-field/id #uuid "898e8b2c-817b-5b6d-b909-be519e9eb032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a049b9-92e8-5818-8daf-281bf457ff15" :question/math-academy-id "q-21047" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=8{x}^{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =16-\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8858be-4fe4-5ebf-be03-cb245cef263a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298939} {:answer/id #uuid "477f16b0-d9d3-5bc6-aa65-b18f291ff324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298940} {:answer/id #uuid "12d78119-1241-5d3b-9cea-1301412362fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298941} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4b95a5-b48b-50f9-a4de-87c5be2b29ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298942} {:answer/id #uuid "fbba851c-96e0-5a8a-a6a2-7cc331323305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No inflection point exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4b95a5-b48b-50f9-a4de-87c5be2b29ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298942} :answer-field/id #uuid "4be8ad13-3a15-5bec-88b9-a5aa40d7a154" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "137cf379-31e0-5aef-a23a-29d963a5bad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-2460" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=0.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6(-1)=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =6(1)=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51bea530-f60f-5ca5-bf53-fb7d5fad6712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298946} {:answer/id #uuid "f21924f5-058e-506d-be3b-867e8b61f62e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298947} {:answer/id #uuid "afb139f0-4170-5a14-84dc-cbb263ad15aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298948} {:answer/id #uuid "95fef0a5-88ac-5c37-bfda-b6906fff3987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298949} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac1fe55-14d6-5ec0-ae84-365d41915a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51bea530-f60f-5ca5-bf53-fb7d5fad6712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298946} :answer-field/id #uuid "579774fb-b006-5be8-ae5a-a8c08dc7c61e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b979a9b-1d53-5944-a4fd-b2809780da32" :question/math-academy-id "q-2465" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-6x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+12x-6 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n12x+12=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)+12=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78c103ee-6059-5303-a35f-46554447d0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298953} {:answer/id #uuid "3a1ed625-4341-58b3-aa10-33f47944f557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298954} {:answer/id #uuid "d5edd98b-8127-5e02-855e-df6e05d9c4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298955} {:answer/id #uuid "34e09784-599c-5cd4-bb0e-ff216bc290e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298956} {:answer/id #uuid "383c080f-9929-598b-93c7-36281679d275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5edd98b-8127-5e02-855e-df6e05d9c4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298955} :answer-field/id #uuid "30be49a0-1ad1-529c-833f-cacee5950bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82843c7f-f0f2-5349-b0a0-01241922fac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-2753" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi \n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\pi .$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\pi ,$ we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "decc4370-a24d-5c18-9598-95f621a07e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298959} {:answer/id #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298960} {:answer/id #uuid "55705a75-81a9-594a-8ad8-92b37266c1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298961} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0b3677-de6c-5e44-a12e-eef28d35cebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298962} {:answer/id #uuid "99a44cc3-3c65-5a9f-abeb-d3b5530603e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298960} :answer-field/id #uuid "236ebe4f-089a-5ef0-a6cc-de2d76cf4d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05af1cea-c88c-5fcc-8838-0055bd7f29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31213" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306164fa-4dd6-5c1a-a107-b6af8267d7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294017} {:answer/id #uuid "66acfaac-58a5-5185-a6d3-c3d0d2c80a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294018} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e389c4-06ed-5dc1-8e71-6f3093c74722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294019} {:answer/id #uuid "09b2ac1d-4454-5f3f-98b2-c98690ed692a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294020} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf65c2b-bc08-5529-b64a-1f85068785ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6,\\,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306164fa-4dd6-5c1a-a107-b6af8267d7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294017} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b053f5-2909-537c-919b-b0d0481d82f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98bca65a-5215-5c3c-9419-26f83fa4001a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294040} {:answer/id #uuid "88841588-0fb1-5108-9559-255db6413849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294040} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f11e8f-09c4-58ef-846e-f3450b62633c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6de3588d-45bf-5afc-a746-6941c52fd3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344063" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2992ee29-95d1-58dc-a11b-42f2c03ce2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298967} {:answer/id #uuid "fd655f84-7eed-549b-8f62-60b2c9c27764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2992ee29-95d1-58dc-a11b-42f2c03ce2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298967} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e3b102-6275-5f93-ab85-bc58a01f6d2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9830b87c-ec53-5f59-89c6-b1659da4f4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-344206" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)=3{x}^{5}+15{x}^{4}-2x+5.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}+60{x}^{3}-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 60{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =60(-4)^{3}+180(-4)^{2}=-960<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =60(-1)^{3}+180(-1)^{2}=120>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =60(1)^{3}+180(1)^{2}=240>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294055} {:answer/id #uuid "722f578c-86db-52bc-9c57-5e83bbf92be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294055} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c0c38c-7dd0-5344-bcb5-cf9e5fb0e6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45f9354a-20d2-5917-8cc8-23e78cc4519e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344246" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0013fc75-105f-51bf-b236-1b455c694d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298972} {:answer/id #uuid "954c1556-51b7-5e5c-a75d-185d2ae85f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$, $x=2$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298973} {:answer/id #uuid "c385bbb3-ad7d-5d2b-a5bd-1e1fe190b479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$, $x=\\pm 2$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298974} {:answer/id #uuid "7683b4df-c3ac-56a9-a427-0f00ca981f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298975} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6e48be-e7ce-5aa7-ae12-adb794b1f3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0013fc75-105f-51bf-b236-1b455c694d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298972} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbe501a8-801b-5b97-9881-09e4fc4d22d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a15760c-3531-560b-9465-64e8a627b33c" :question/math-academy-id "q-51253" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/7d71b69f27d14ca6c9ff0467ecce1df7be2f53684b162a316dc8877218b202ca.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-2$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=-2,$   the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f15cb921-e01e-5c44-b92c-65e3aa1dc732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298979} {:answer/id #uuid "546e66be-e783-5646-9a01-c528d19bdafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298980} {:answer/id #uuid "b0da4bd6-15f6-5ded-ad62-6714c3255db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298981} {:answer/id #uuid "eb45b26b-5768-57de-97fe-6add6959626a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298982} {:answer/id #uuid "bec13d20-7547-5fcb-a8a2-62591c09858b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0da4bd6-15f6-5ded-ad62-6714c3255db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298981} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2cac989-c353-5a27-ab6f-0157deef9472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d4e35db-d1ef-591b-b705-c5eebc3ef913" :question/math-academy-id "q-51423" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/c19b9819f3fa99aafcbc88e1b020bef760891bcffdf3a8ae7b1ed50dd657655c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$  is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=1.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065deb0c-5d57-546a-8c8d-c914f8b17320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095786} {:answer/id #uuid "2b03460f-477c-5b1d-b368-fe255ea85a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095787} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c35f0-de90-5a67-88a5-46ccd8b6d36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095788} {:answer/id #uuid "532d9d26-989b-5702-a201-7fe2e4b78002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095789} {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b35f28-2017-55f5-b4dc-61220e3e94f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2792f399-d353-50d3-affd-f9260fbd9716" :question/math-academy-id "q-51428" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed: \n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=5$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=5.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11afb0da-5f86-5fbf-9002-6b4185d8b7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298986} {:answer/id #uuid "a133126a-3a03-5f98-a4f9-aecfd33bec13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298987} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d6c918-da41-51bc-a297-cccc3e1df1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298988} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c8d33e-02d7-5634-9e94-8b53e23658dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298989} {:answer/id #uuid "dc772939-2811-5959-9982-9681d4dbf135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc772939-2811-5959-9982-9681d4dbf135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298990} :answer-field/id #uuid "10d62fd4-a3a3-59db-bf3c-405ceb1d6f48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf542050-9ffb-55f9-a8d4-4542e17194b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79199" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/dba173a7cf462e7e67067a78c12ea05a50ea5713da0e6ddaed152055b3ce589f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at  $x=-2,$  $x=0,$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$"}]}
